// Data model: theorems and conjectures of arithmetic geometry and analytic number theory around the
// Riemann hypothesis, the Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture and the abc conjecture.
//
// Basic model as in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js, extended by conjectures:
//   nodes type "grundlage": assumed knowledge outside this graph.
//              type "satz": proved theorem. `formula` (LaTeX), `text` (HTML), `area`, `year`.
//              type "vermutung": unproved statement with field `state` (key from `statuses`).
//              Optional: `ag` (id of the same theorem in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js),
//              `large` (id in ../Große Sätze und Vermutungen/daten.js), `gallery` (id in ../Mathematical
//              Wonder/daten.js).
//              `msc`: three-digit classes of the Mathematics Subject Classification 2020, most important first; a
//              second
//              class only if the literature clearly also places the theorem there. `field`: the corresponding
//              top-level numbers (11 number theory, 14 algebraic geometry, 22 topological groups and Lie groups,
//              30 functions of a complex variable, 42 harmonic analysis, 46 functional analysis, 68 computer science).
//              The
//              assignment is our own judgment and is not taken from databases such as zbMATH.
//              Optional `province`: map location (drafts 7, 8) when it differs from the first class in `msc`; for
//              instance theorems whose main class is 11F80 (Galois representations) lie in 11R (decision on B24 b).
//              `form` and `short`: logical form and statement in one sentence of prose (TODO B26); `formula` is the
//              quantifier-free core. Optional `credit: { person, year }`: the originators shown in line 2 of the
//              card when they differ from the first event „bewiesen“ (or „vermutet“ for conjectures).
//              Foundations (hill ranges) may have a field `rangeList`: the fixed list of names of contained results
//              that `rangeItems` of a proof may use for this premise (B42, working assumption F15.9).
//              Optional `correspondence` (B24 b; on a theorem or conjecture that relates two independently defined
//              sides, drawn as a bridge instead of a mountain from draft 10 on; every correspondence is a bridge of its
//              own, also a proved special case of a larger one and a construction in one direction with preserved
//              L-function): { sides: [{ name, objects, msc (five-character MSC classes, optional), provinces
//              (provinces of the map) }, …], mapping (optional), preserved: { text, formula (optional) }, inscription
//              (optional), direction, dictionary: [{ side1, side2 }, …] (optional; corresponding notions of the first
//              and second side) }. `direction` (code, not translated): "both" (a bijection or a correspondence proved
//              in both directions), "galois-to-automorphic" (to every object of the Galois side an automorphic one is
//              found) or "automorphic-to-galois" (to every automorphic object a Galois one is constructed). For the
//              Langlands correspondences the first side is the Galois side (provinces 11R, 11S) and the second the
//              automorphic side (11F, 22E); the bridges stand over the same fjord.
//              `generality` (integer, optional): rank of the correspondences over the same fjord by how much they
//              bridge, 1 = narrowest. Ordered first by the group (GL<sub>1</sub> < GL<sub>2</sub> < GL<sub>n</sub> <
//              reductive G), then by the class of objects and fields covered (special class < all of a kind; ℚ <
//              number fields; one direction < both directions where comparable). Narrow bridges stand near the inner
//              end of the fjord, general ones near the mouth (user, 2026-10-20).
//   proofs Hyperedges: premises (jointly required, AND) -> conclusion `from`. Several proofs of the same statement
//              are alternatives (OR).
//              Optional `shortcut: true`: abbreviated copy of a proof that stands in full in
//              ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie (node with field `ag`). This graph shows it so that the theorem has a
//              logical depth; the map (maps/scripts/entwurf-7-modell.js) skips it and draws the full proof from there.
//              Optional field `rangeItems: { <premise id>: ["short name", ...] }`: for premises that point to a
//              foundation (a presupposed theory, on the map a building and later a hill range), the results of that
//              theory the proof actually uses, e.g. `rangeItems: { G_ANT: ["Einheitensatz"] }`. If such a result
//              later becomes a node of its own, every proof that names it can be re-pointed without checking the
//              proof again (TODO B37, B41). Premises that are theorems need no entry. Filled for the proofs created or
//              changed since B41 (20 October 2026); older proofs do not have it yet. If the foundation has a field
//              `rangeList`, every name comes from that list.
//              Field `kind`:
//                "beweis" ordinary proof; all premises are theorems or foundations.
//                "bedingt" proof with at least one conjecture among its premises. The conclusion is then only
//                          proved under this conjecture (or, for a theorem already proved, by a second route).
//   relations Binary relations that are not proofs. Field `kind`:
//                "teilresultat" `from` (theorem) is a proved special case or a weakened form of `to`
//                                (conjecture). The theorem is not a premise of the conjecture.
//                "aequivalent" It is proved that `from` and `to` are equivalent. `evidence` names the proof.
//                "analogie" Transfer between two fields, not a logical dependency.
//                "formulierung" `from` (theorem) must be known for the statement `to` to make sense at all,
//                                e.g. because `to` speaks of a function value that only `from` defines.
//                "widerlegt" `from` (counterexample) disproves `to` (a conjecture or a conjectured principle).
//                "deutung" `from` (theorem) shows what the statement `to` means without proving it; e.g.:
//                                the Tate–Shafarevich group measures the failure of the Hasse principle.
//
// Counterexamples (same model in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js):
//   nodes type "gegenbeispiel": a concrete object that disproves a statement, together with the proof that
//                it does so. It is proved and, like a theorem, has a logical depth; its proofs are ordinary
//                proofs. Every counterexample has at least one relation "widerlegt". The disproved statement is a
//                conjecture with status "widerlegt".
//
// Why two lists: a proof is a hyperedge with several premises; partial result, equivalence, analogy and
// prerequisite of the formulation are relations between exactly two statements and not derivations of the target
// statement.
// If they were entered as proofs, a partial result would look like a premise of the conjecture.
//
// Logical depth: it is computed only via proofs of kind "beweis". Conjectures have no logical depth; they
// are placed in separate columns on the right (see index.html).
//
// AI proofs (same model in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js; display in graph.js there, GL.ai…):
//   proofs Optional field `ki`: according to those involved, the proof comes entirely or substantially from a
//                language model. { company, model, share ("vollständig" | "wesentlich" | "Mithilfe"), date (of the
//                manuscript),
//                people: [{ name, role }], verification ("unbestätigt" | "in Prüfung" | "vom Autor geprüft" |
//                "von Fachleuten bestätigt" | "zurückgezogen"), lean ("vollständig" | "teilweise" | "keine"),
//                source (address), state (date of the entry), note (optional) }.
//                New proof kind "beansprucht": proof with field `ki` that no experts have confirmed. Like
//                "bedingt", it does not count toward logical depth. Its premises are theorems, foundations or nodes
//                with status
//                "beansprucht". An AI proof that a mathematician has checked and published as author is an
//                ordinary proof (kind "beweis") with field `ki`.
//   nodes Status "beansprucht" for a statement whose proof is only claimed. Such a statement is a
//                node of type "vermutung", so that it gets no logical depth and nothing appears as proved.
//                The statuses of existing conjectures stay unchanged, even if an AI proof is claimed for them.
//                `ki: true` on nodes, relations and events: in the graph only because of an AI result; they
//                appear only with the switch „KI-Beweise“ (address ki=1).
//   relations "teilresultat" may also start at a node with status "beansprucht".
//   history Events "beansprucht" (manuscript submitted) and "zurueckgezogen" (withdrawn by the originator).
//   Sources and classification: ../Materialsammlung/Von KI bewiesene Sätze in algebraischer und arithmetischer
//   Geometrie.md.
//
// Methods (same model in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js; map draft: canals, see maps/Konzept/09):
//   methods A method is a reusable procedure that makes a certain kind of proof step routinely possible
//                that previously required an idea of its own (examples: sieve methods, deformation theory,
//                point counting with the Lefschetz trace formula). It is not a theorem: a theorem enters a proof as a
//                premise and is listed in `premises`; a method describes how the proof proceeds.
//                Nor is it a whole theory with its own concepts and theorems; such theories appear as
//                foundations or areas in the graph. A theorem can be a premise and at the same time found a method
//                (modularity lifting, Lefschetz trace formula).
//                { id ("M_…"), name, text, source, sourceFolder, year, circa (optional, year approximate), person:
//                [...],
//                msc (optional, MSC 2020 classes) }. `source` is the id of the node in which the method originated, if
//                it occurs in one of the two graphs, otherwise null. `sourceFolder` says in which graph this
//                node is: "AG" (../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie, daten.js and daten-gross.js) or "AR"
//                (../Beispiel Arithmetische Geometrie); null as well if source is null. A method that occurs in both
//                folders has the same id and the same entry there, so that the map can merge them.
//                Each folder lists only the methods that its proofs use. Only methods are included
//                that at least two proofs of the two graphs together use; a proof that appears identically in both
//                folders
//                (node with field `ag`) counts once.
//   proofs Optional field `methods: [<id>, ...]`: methods that the proof uses. The basis is the proof idea
//                (`idea`) and the literature cited there. If the field is missing, no method is entered; this does
//                not mean that the proof uses none.
//   Display in the info box: GL.methodsHtml in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/graph.js.
//
// Classification theorems (same model in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/daten.js):
//   nodes Optional field `classification: { objects, classes }` on theorems that divide all objects of a kind
//   completely
//                into classes or determine them up to isomorphism by invariants (example: Enriques–Kodaira
//                classification; objects "minimale glatte projektive Flächen über ℂ", classes "nach der
//                Kodaira-Dimension …"). Both fields are HTML text. A criterion for a single property, an
//                equivalence of categories or the existence of a moduli space does not count as a classification here.
//                The map shows classification theorems as a tree (user's decision of 20 October 2026).
//                Display in the info box: GL.classificationHtml in ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/graph.js.
//
// Sources for the proof routes: Davenport, Multiplicative Number Theory (3rd ed. 2000); Titchmarsh, The Theory of the
// Riemann Zeta-Function (2nd ed. 1986); Iwaniec and Kowalski, Analytic Number Theory (2004); Silverman, The
// Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves (1986, 2009); Darmon, Diamond and Taylor, Fermat's Last Theorem (1995); Wiles, The
// Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer Conjecture (Clay, 2000); Bombieri, Problems of the Millennium: The Riemann Hypothesis
// (Clay, 2000); Bombieri and Gubler, Heights in Diophantine Geometry (2006); Granville and Tucker, It's as easy as abc
// (Notices AMS 2002); Kleiman, Algebraic cycles and the Weil conjectures (1968); Conrey, The Riemann Hypothesis
// (Notices AMS 2003); Broughan, Equivalents of the Riemann Hypothesis (2017).
// All years are taken from secondary knowledge and have not yet been checked against sources.

